From b354e195b9675a07bce2f8a6848a1d0ea769a8d0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mukayi <1515982581@qq.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 19:34:24 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 02/12] fix(metrics): reuse one HTTP connection and keep
 /report_step off the event loop

The metrics service is enabled by default (`--use-metrics-service` defaults to
True), which puts synchronous HTTP on the training thread and makes the
logging rank arrive late at the next collective.

Client side
-----------
`MetricsClient` called the module-level `requests.post` / `requests.get` for
every request, so no connection was ever reused: one TCP handshake per metric
batch, per report step, per rollout and per optimizer step, all on the rank
that also has to reach the grad-sync reduce-scatter. Hold one
`requests.Session` for the lifetime of the client instead.

The same session sets `trust_env = False`. The metrics service is a
cluster-local endpoint, and honoring `HTTP_PROXY` / `HTTPS_PROXY` from the
environment would route those requests through a proxy -- the sibling
`sglang_exporter` already does this.

The eleven `print()` calls in the module become logger warnings, per the
project logging convention.

Service side
------------
`/report_step` was declared `async def` but ran its backend fan-out inline:
`wandb.log`, `SummaryWriter.add_scalar`, the ClearML adapter and the timeline
`json.dump` all block. This deployment is a single replica, so a slow backend
stalled every `/log_metrics_batch` that arrived meanwhile -- the endpoint
itself is cheap, its neighbours are what got slow.

Move the fan-out into `_report_to_backends`, a plain synchronous method, and
await it through `asyncio.to_thread`, serialized by a lock because none of the
backends documents thread safety and reporting is already sequential per step.
The package already uses `asyncio.to_thread` for its own parse/render work in
`sglang_exporter`.

Tests
-----
`tests/utils/test_metrics_client_transport.py` covers what the existing tests
could not: they patch the module-level `requests` functions, so they never
exercise the real transport. The new tests drive a real HTTP server and count
accepted connections (one, not six), assert a proxy in the environment does
not capture the request, and check that a deliberately slow backend leaves the
event loop free to run other work.

